    <innerclass refid="structOpenSkyhawk_1_1LinkRecovery" prot="public">OpenSkyhawk::LinkRecovery</innerclass>
    <innernamespace refid="namespaceOpenSkyhawk">OpenSkyhawk</innernamespace>
    <briefdescription>
<para>USB self-recovery ladder for <ref refid="namespaceSimGateway" kindref="compound">SimGateway</ref> — pure decision logic, no hardware. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The <ref refid="namespaceSimGateway" kindref="compound">SimGateway</ref> is powered only from its USB-C, so while it runs it is plugged into something. Running but not configured by a host is therefore abnormal, and the failure this exists for (#355) looks exactly like that: the device dropped off the bus while the core kept running, and only a power cycle brought it back. The ladder escalates:</para>
<para><orderedlist>
<listitem><para>unhealthy for LINK_BAD_MS → detach, then attach LINK_REATTACH_GAP_MS later (toggles the D+ pull-up: the host sees a fresh plug-in)</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>still unhealthy LINK_REBOOT_AFTER_MS → reboot (fully resets the USB controller)</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>after a recovery reboot → re-attach every LINK_BACKOFF_MS, never reboot again until the link has been healthy once</para>
</listitem></orderedlist>
</para>
<para>Step 3 keeps a <ref refid="namespaceSimGateway" kindref="compound">SimGateway</ref> on a charger, or back-fed with no host, from reboot-looping.</para>
<para>Pure: takes <computeroutput>now</computeroutput> and the sampled health, returns what to do. <ref refid="SimGateway_8cpp" kindref="compound">SimGateway.cpp</ref> applies the action, so SIMGATEWAY_TEST builds drive the whole ladder with injected inputs.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="copyright"><para>GPL-2.0-only — see Firmware/LICENSE </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
